{
  "image_name": "0a40a91128381bc0832927bc2e56c1df.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes different engineered agonists that activate the FGF21 receptor system and compares how they engage its two components, FGFR1 and β‑Klotho. A small schematic at the top left shows the natural receptor complex, while the central diagram organizes many drug designs around a large shape divided into regions representing binding to both targets, to β‑Klotho alone, or to FGFR1 alone.\n\nMost molecules in the upper half are modified FGF21 proteins that still appear to bind both FGFR1 and β‑Klotho. These include PEGylated forms, Fc fusions, antibody-linked versions, and other long-acting analogues, each labeled with a reported half-life that generally increases from a few hours for native-like constructs to tens of hours for engineered versions. In contrast, the lower half contains antibody-based receptor agonists. Some are bispecific or biparatopic formats that mainly target β‑Klotho, some target FGFR1, and others combine both specificities in one antibody scaffold.\n\nOverall, the graphic conveys that FGF21 signaling can be mimicked through several molecular architectures, and that engineering the ligand or using antibodies changes receptor-binding mode and substantially extends pharmacokinetic half-life.",
